<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Peace of Montreal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1701">1701</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> was a peace treaty signed in August 1701 by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, Louis-Hector de Callière, and the representatives of about forty Native nations, among them the Iroquois Confederacy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s Native allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>. It ended nearly a century of intermittent war, and the Iroquois promised to stay neutral in conflicts between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Over 1,300 delegates attended the conference.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-peace-of-montreal.jpg" alt="Great Peace of Montreal" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Secrétaire de Callière et autre main (pour les pictogrammes) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Paix_Montreal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a>, fought for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> founded Quebec City in 1608. The Hurons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algonquin-people">Algonquins</a>, backed by the French, faced the Haudenosaunee of the League of Five Nations, backed first by New Netherland and later by the English. In 1666 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carignan-salieres-regiment">Carignan-Salières Regiment</a> devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> communities, but the Haudenosaunee went on expanding westward. After their raid on Lachine in 1689, Governor Frontenac sent raids against the English frontier, and the border war formally ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> in 1697, which left Haudenosaunee sovereignty unresolved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>French allies in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> kept raiding deep into Haudenosaunee territory, and the English could not protect them. Weakened by war and epidemics, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> collapsing as prices fell, the Haudenosaunee pursued peace. Talks in 1698 and 1699 were hampered by the English, but a first conference was held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> in March 1700, and in September a preliminary treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> with the five Haudenosaunee nations.</p><p>All the nations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> were then invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> for the summer of 1701, and delegations arrived after long journeys. Talks with Governor Callière dragged on as both sides bargained. The document was signed on a field prepared outside the city, each nation's representatives placing their clan's symbol, such as a turtle, wolf or bear, at its foot. A banquet followed, at which the chiefs shared a peace pipe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Negotiated according to First Nations diplomatic custom, the treaty put negotiation in place of direct conflict, with the French as arbiters between the signatory nations, and for those nations it established the Tree of Peace among all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> peoples. Trade and exploration resumed, and Antoine Laumet de La Mothe, Sieur de Cadillac, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> to found Fort Pontchartrain du Détroit, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a>. The peace was fragile: within three years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a> attacked a Haudenosaunee village near a French fort at present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingston-ontario">Kingston, Ontario</a>.</p><p>The treaty is still considered valid by the Indigenous nations involved. A square in Old Montreal was renamed Place de la Grande-Paix-de-Montréal for its 300th anniversary, and the Kondiaronk Belvedere honours the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petun">Petun</a> leader Kondiaronk, one of its architects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Peace of Montreal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Exhausted by these wars, the league made the <b>Great Peace of Montreal</b> with France and its Native allies in 1701.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Peace of Montreal</b> of 1701, signed by about forty Native nations, ended a century of war with the Iroquois.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the mid-1670s they were allied with the English in the Covenant Chain, and in 1701 they made the <b>Great Peace of Montreal</b> with the French.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
